1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,696
At nine in the morning,
one student hears a rumour.

2
00:00:03,696 --> 00:00:07,322
Every hour, each person who
knows it tells one new friend.

3
00:00:07,722 --> 00:00:09,944
Aarav worked it out on his bicycle.

4
00:00:09,944 --> 00:00:12,911
One person, then two,
then four, then eight.

5
00:00:13,361 --> 00:00:17,741
By seven in the evening, ten hours
later, one thousand and twenty four

6
00:00:17,741 --> 00:00:18,679
people know.

7
00:00:19,029 --> 00:00:21,401
Adding the same number grows slowly.

8
00:00:21,401 --> 00:00:23,261
Multiplying grows like this.

9
00:00:23,761 --> 00:00:27,665
A list that multiplies by the same
number every time is called a geometric

10
00:00:27,665 --> 00:00:28,565
progression.

11
00:00:29,089 --> 00:00:30,542
Compare it with an AP.

12
00:00:30,542 --> 00:00:32,877
There, we add the same number each time.

13
00:00:32,877 --> 00:00:34,437
Two, four, six, eight.

14
00:00:34,837 --> 00:00:39,348
In a geometric progression, or GP,
we multiply by the same number each

15
00:00:39,348 --> 00:00:40,248
time.

16
00:00:39,893 --> 00:00:41,924
Two, four, eight, sixteen.

17
00:00:42,324 --> 00:00:43,727
Here is the test.

18
00:00:43,727 --> 00:00:46,803
Divide any term by the
term just before it.

19
00:00:46,803 --> 00:00:50,329
If the answer is always
the same, it is a GP.

20
00:00:50,729 --> 00:00:52,378
Four over two is two.

21
00:00:52,378 --> 00:00:54,130
Eight over four is two.

22
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:56,037
Sixteen over eight is two.

23
00:00:56,437 --> 00:00:59,603
That fixed answer is
called the common ratio, r.

24
00:00:59,603 --> 00:01:01,489
The first term is called a.

25
00:01:01,489 --> 00:01:03,481
Neither a nor r can be zero.

26
00:01:04,131 --> 00:01:05,850
Now watch what r does.

27
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:08,653
We keep a as one, and change r live.

28
00:01:09,053 --> 00:01:12,293
When r is two, each bar
is double the one before.

29
00:01:12,293 --> 00:01:13,641
The list shoots up.

30
00:01:14,041 --> 00:01:16,345
When r is one, nothing changes.

31
00:01:16,345 --> 00:01:17,701
One, one, one.

32
00:01:18,101 --> 00:01:21,508
When r is a half, each bar
is half the one before.

33
00:01:21,508 --> 00:01:24,838
The list shrinks towards
zero, but never reaches it.

34
00:01:25,238 --> 00:01:28,561
And when r is minus two,
the sign flips every time.

35
00:01:28,561 --> 00:01:31,529
Up, down, up, down, and bigger each time.

36
00:01:32,179 --> 00:01:34,447
Now, how do we jump straight to any term?

37
00:01:34,847 --> 00:01:35,747
Step one.

38
00:01:35,509 --> 00:01:36,760
The first term is a.

39
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:38,410
Step two.

40
00:01:38,215 --> 00:01:40,039
The second term is a times r.

41
00:01:40,789 --> 00:01:41,689
Step three.

42
00:01:41,639 --> 00:01:44,033
The third term is a times r times r.

43
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:45,465
That is a r squared.

44
00:01:46,215 --> 00:01:47,115
Step four.

45
00:01:47,036 --> 00:01:50,105
Each term has one r fewer
than its place number.

46
00:01:50,105 --> 00:01:53,343
So term n is a times r
to the power n minus one.

47
00:01:54,093 --> 00:01:56,874
For the rumour, a is one and r is two.

48
00:01:56,874 --> 00:01:58,582
Hour ten is term eleven.

49
00:01:58,582 --> 00:02:01,998
Two to the power ten, one
thousand and twenty four.

50
00:02:02,648 --> 00:02:03,689
Side by side.

51
00:02:03,689 --> 00:02:05,492
An AP adds d each time.

52
00:02:05,492 --> 00:02:07,874
Its bars climb like a straight ramp.

53
00:02:08,274 --> 00:02:10,270
AGP multiplies by r each time.

54
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:13,577
Its bars climb along a curve
that gets steeper and steeper.

55
00:02:14,177 --> 00:02:15,077
Your turn.

56
00:02:15,022 --> 00:02:18,789
Is three, six, twelve,
twenty four an AP or a GP?

57
00:02:18,789 --> 00:02:19,984
Pause and think.

58
00:02:23,184 --> 00:02:24,084
AGP.

59
00:02:23,921 --> 00:02:27,465
Six over three is two, and
twelve over six is two.

60
00:02:27,465 --> 00:02:30,535
The gaps are not equal,
but the ratios are.

61
00:02:31,185 --> 00:02:32,330
Remember this.

62
00:02:32,330 --> 00:02:37,692
In a GP, every term divided by the
one before gives the same number, r.

63
00:02:38,392 --> 00:02:40,082
Now, one worked example.

64
00:02:40,432 --> 00:02:44,746
Which term of three, six, twelve,
and so on, is seven hundred and sixty

65
00:02:44,746 --> 00:02:45,646
eight?

66
00:02:45,790 --> 00:02:46,690
Step one.

67
00:02:46,642 --> 00:02:47,661
a is three.

68
00:02:47,661 --> 00:02:50,290
r is six over three, which is two.

69
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:51,940
Step two.

70
00:02:51,797 --> 00:02:55,045
Term n is three times two
to the power n minus one.

71
00:02:55,045 --> 00:02:57,896
Set it equal to seven
hundred and sixty eight.

72
00:02:58,646 --> 00:02:59,576
Step three.

73
00:02:59,576 --> 00:03:00,821
Divide by three.

74
00:03:00,821 --> 00:03:04,797
Two to the power n minus one
is two hundred and fifty six.

75
00:03:05,547 --> 00:03:06,447
Step four.

76
00:03:06,352 --> 00:03:09,599
Two hundred and fifty six
is two to the power eight.

77
00:03:09,599 --> 00:03:11,274
So n minus one is eight.

78
00:03:12,024 --> 00:03:13,166
So n is nine.

79
00:03:13,166 --> 00:03:16,363
Seven hundred and sixty
eight is the ninth term.

80
00:03:17,013 --> 00:03:18,475
Now, the exam trap.

81
00:03:18,825 --> 00:03:21,417
Find the sixth term of five, ten, twenty.

82
00:03:21,417 --> 00:03:24,419
Many students write five
times two to the power six.

83
00:03:24,819 --> 00:03:27,248
But the first term has no r at all.

84
00:03:27,248 --> 00:03:29,074
Term six has only five r's.

85
00:03:29,074 --> 00:03:32,077
Five times thirty two is
one hundred and sixty.

86
00:03:32,727 --> 00:03:34,166
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:34,166 --> 00:03:35,161
In JEE Main.

88
00:03:35,161 --> 00:03:39,962
In April twenty sixteen, three terms
of an AP formed a GP, and the paper

89
00:03:39,962 --> 00:03:41,798
asked for its common ratio.

90
00:03:42,398 --> 00:03:43,554
Exam level now.

91
00:03:43,554 --> 00:03:48,161
In a GP of positive terms, the
second plus the third term is twelve.

92
00:03:48,161 --> 00:03:51,190
The fourth plus the fifth
term is forty eight.

93
00:03:51,190 --> 00:03:52,749
Find the seventh term.

94
00:03:52,749 --> 00:03:53,955
Pause and solve.

95
00:03:59,355 --> 00:04:00,730
The answer is B.

96
00:04:00,730 --> 00:04:02,863
One hundred and twenty eight.

97
00:04:03,363 --> 00:04:08,212
The fourth and fifth terms are the
second and third terms, times r squared.

98
00:04:08,212 --> 00:04:11,903
So r squared is forty eight
over twelve, which is four.

99
00:04:12,403 --> 00:04:14,741
The terms are positive, so r is two.

100
00:04:15,241 --> 00:04:19,689
Then a r plus a r squared is two
a plus four a, which is six a.

101
00:04:19,689 --> 00:04:21,933
Six a is twelve, so a is two.

102
00:04:21,933 --> 00:04:26,540
The seventh term is two times sixty
four, one hundred and twenty eight.

103
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:28,680
Here is your recap.

104
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:30,262
Screenshot this card.

105
00:04:33,462 --> 00:04:35,648
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

106
00:04:36,048 --> 00:04:36,948
One.

107
00:04:36,482 --> 00:04:38,746
How do you test if a list is a GP?

108
00:04:39,646 --> 00:04:40,546
Two.

109
00:04:40,079 --> 00:04:43,316
Find the eighth term of
one, three, nine, and so on.

110
00:04:44,216 --> 00:04:45,116
Three.

111
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:48,941
Why does the n th term have r
to the power n minus one, not n?

112
00:04:50,041 --> 00:04:52,330
Next time: grains on a chessboard.

113
00:04:52,330 --> 00:04:54,125
One, two, four, eight.

114
00:04:54,125 --> 00:04:55,740
How many grains in all?

115
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:57,126
The sum of a GP.
